<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Lippe</h1><p class="st-meta">1123 CE – 1789 CE (approx.) · capital Detmold</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Westphalian lordship around the Teutoburg Forest whose rulers founded Lippstadt and Lemgo and survived as a German state until 1947.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bernhard I appears as lord of Lippe in 1123, holding lands on the Lippe river. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernhard-ii-of-lippe">Bernhard II</a>, a companion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lippstadt">Lippstadt</a> around 1185 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemgo">Lemgo</a>, before becoming a crusader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-confederation">Livonia</a>. The lordship developed between the bishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Lippe was a rural territory of small towns and farms in the hills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a>. The lords were raised to counts in 1528 and introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, the Reformed faith following in 1605. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detmold">Detmold</a> became the main residence. Branches split off, including <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-schaumburg-lippe">Schaumburg-Lippe</a> in 1647.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-august-count-of-lippe-detmold">Simon August</a>'s successor Leopold I was raised to prince in 1789, making the county the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-lippe">Principality of Lippe</a>. It joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire</a>, and became a Free State in 1918 before merging into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a> in 1947.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-lippe.jpg" alt="County of Lippe" width="320" height="290"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_County_of_Lippe_(1560).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1123 CE – 1789 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Detmold</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bernhard II of Lippe, Simon VI of Lippe, Simon August of Lippe</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lippe-Detmold, Lordship of Lippe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1456&amp;focus=county-of-lippe" aria-label="County of Lippe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4880 382 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Lippe lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5200 638L5229 694M5200 638L5200 638"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5200 638h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5200 638h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5200 638h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Detmold. <a href="/atlas?year=1456&amp;focus=county-of-lippe">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
